WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.424 --> 00:00:10.260
I study the dynamics of planetary systems.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:11.344
I'm an astronomer.

00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:14.472
I focus especially on native
and indigenous literatures

00:00:14.472 --> 00:00:16.391
of the United States.

00:00:16.391 --> 00:00:17.809
I'm a literary scholar.

00:00:17.809 --> 00:00:21.771
My research has a lot to do
with the institutions of democracy.

00:00:21.771 --> 00:00:23.231
And I'm a political theorist.

00:00:23.231 --> 00:00:28.319
Astronomy is this really amazing field
because by looking up and understanding

00:00:28.319 --> 00:00:31.614
the stars and the cosmos in this greater
context, it really gives us

00:00:31.614 --> 00:00:34.367
a better sense of our own planet

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:35.827
and our place in the universe.

00:00:35.827 --> 00:00:36.411
In a lot of ways,

00:00:36.411 --> 00:00:38.997
when you step back, it's
a very existential field.

00:00:38.997 --> 00:00:40.290
I'm interested academically

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.877
in how people relate
to their environments and to their places.

00:00:43.877 --> 00:00:45.420
How do places own us?

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.964
How do we own places
and belong within them?

00:00:47.964 --> 00:00:49.299
One thing we want to do is

00:00:49.299 --> 00:00:53.053
we want to understand
how the world that we see is put together.

00:00:53.053 --> 00:00:57.348
Political theory helps us to see beyond
what is immediately apparent.

00:00:57.348 --> 00:01:01.686
When the job ad came up
for coming to Yale, it was my dream job.

00:01:01.686 --> 00:01:05.732
I feel very privileged to have access
to all of the incredible resources.

00:01:05.732 --> 00:01:11.154
It just felt like a great place to do
research at this early part of my career

00:01:11.154 --> 00:01:12.655
where I knew I'd be supported.

00:01:12.655 --> 00:01:14.741
One of the things that I most value

00:01:14.741 --> 00:01:18.328
is the students who come from
all over the world, all kinds of backgrounds.

00:01:18.328 --> 00:01:21.247
I can now directly support students,
and that is really

00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:24.292
the core of my job,
is to uplift other people.

00:01:24.292 --> 00:01:28.588
I will ask my students to try to loosen
up performativity

00:01:28.588 --> 00:01:35.220
and instead just kind of come into a
sincere approach to the books that we study.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:36.596
And I also hope they know
that I'm interested

00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:38.598
in getting to know them as humans, too.

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:40.517
What you get out of classes at Yale

00:01:40.517 --> 00:01:42.477
 is going to have a lot to do
with what you put into them.

00:01:42.477 --> 00:01:47.148
I think it's just the best place that
I could be starting out my academic career.

00:01:47.148 --> 00:01:48.983
It  has really come to feel like home.

00:01:48.983 --> 00:01:53.321
I love literature and I hope that
my energy in the classroom is infectious.

00:01:53.321 --> 00:01:54.656
My name is Malena Rice.

00:01:54.656 --> 00:01:57.117
And I'm an assistant professor
in the Department of Astronomy.

00:01:57.117 --> 00:01:58.326
My name is Lloyd Sy.

00:01:58.326 --> 00:02:01.496
I'm a new assistant professor of English.

00:02:01.496 --> 00:02:02.831
My name is Kevin Elliot.

00:02:02.831 --> 00:02:03.790
I am lecturer

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:06.084
in Ethics, Politics and Economics.

